  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x and z from −2 to 2. It is steeper in x, so overshooting shows there first. Steps diverge when learning rate × 3 > 2, so above about 0.67.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with spread 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b. It is centred on the true slope and exactly zero at 64.",
    "Arrows are drawn at their true length (scale 1), so the orange tip is where the ball goes next. Grey shows the full-data step −learning rate × true slope. Both are hidden when a run ends.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or is marked diverged once the loss passes 6 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. The ball is then shown at the bowl's edge, but the loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "The trail holds the start point plus one point per step (up to 41). The ghost keeps the last run that had steps. Noise uses a fixed seed and a run counter, so Reset replays the same noise.",
    "One update happens every 4 host steps (7.5 updates a second at speed 1). Changing a setting mid-run affects only the next steps."
  ]
}